section("abstract_structured.md", [
("H","# Abstract"),
("H","**Background**"),
(KEEP,"FVB mice are widely used in transgenic and disease-model research, but routine RNA-seq commonly uses C57BL/6J-derived reference genomes, which may introduce strain-dependent mapping bias."),
("H","**Methods**"),
("CORR","We surveyed public GEO series containing FVB RNA-seq data, analysed allele-level bias in two FVB × C57BL/6 F1 hybrid datasets, and processed FVB/NJ versus C57BL/6J RNA-seq data from four tissues of two laboratories («n_animals_strain_comparison_FVB|d» FVB/NJ and «n_animals_strain_comparison_B6|d» C57BL/6J animals), every sample verified by a sequence-based strain-identity check, with a B6-congenic tumour dataset as a negative control, through naive B6, WASP-filtered B6, and FVB/NJ personalised reference alignment."),
("H","**Results**"),
("CORR/PHASE-K1","Among «survey_stated_B6_n|d» studies reporting a reference genome, all used a C57BL/6J-derived assembly and none reported strain-aware alignment in public metadata. In F1 hybrids, naive B6 alignment reduced the mean FVB allele fraction to «f1_naive_pooled_mean|.3f»; WASP overcorrected it to «f1_wasp_pooled_mean|.3f». In pure-strain comparisons, reference choice had little transcriptome-wide effect, and the genome-wide bias distribution was centered near zero in every tissue. We identified a conservative «catalog_n|d»-gene catalog. All pure-strain animals were FVB/NJ, so the catalog is substrain-matched throughout. Catalog genes were enriched for FVB/NJ SNP density. In pure-strain analyses, WASP changed genome-wide mean log₂ fold changes by at most «gw_wasp_minus_naive_absmax|.3f» units, consistent with low SNP density across most transcribed regions rather than algorithm failure. At the most SNP-dense loci, however, a SNP-only personalised reference is not sufficient: at *Rps2* in skin the apparent «ex_extreme_naive_fold|.0f»-fold difference disappears through loss of C57BL/6J reads rather than recovery of FVB/NJ reads under unique-only counting."),
("H","**Conclusions**"),
(KEEP,"B6-reference alignment does not broadly distort FVB RNA-seq data, but it can substantially underestimate FVB expression at a small number of SNP-dense loci. The catalog and three-arm framework provide a practical resource for interpreting FVB RNA-seq studies and avoiding artefact-driven animal experiments."),
])
section("keywords.md", [("H","***Keywords***"),("NEW-ID","FVB; C57BL/6J; RNA-seq; reference-genome mapping bias; SNP; personalised reference; WASP; g2gtools; sample identity verification")])
# ============================================================ BACKGROUND (J-1) ===============================================
section("introduction.md", [
("H","# Background"),
(KEEP,"RNA sequencing (RNA-seq) is now a standard approach for transcriptome-wide gene expression analysis in mouse models, and its quantitative accuracy depends on the reference genome used for read alignment. This issue is particularly relevant to inbred mouse strains, which are genetically diverse despite the continued use of a single C57BL/6J (B6)-based reference genome in most RNA-seq workflows."),
("R1-M1","The Mouse Genomes Project has shown that laboratory mouse strains carry millions of single-nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs), short indels, and structural variants relative to the B6 reference assembly [CITE Keane 2011, Lilue 2018]. For FVB/NJ the scale of the divergence is substantial: the Mouse Genomes Project calls about «vcf_fvbnj_homalt_autosomal_snps|M» homozygous autosomal SNPs and «vcf_fvbnj_indels|M» indels relative to GRCm39 in this strain [CITE Doran 2016], «vcf_fvbnj_homalt_exonic_snps|,d» of the SNPs within annotated exons, although they are far from uniformly distributed, so that many genes carry no variant at all while others carry tens of SNPs per kilobase. Reads from a non-reference strain can therefore contain alleles that are absent from the reference. When such reads are aligned to the B6 genome, they may align with lower confidence, align to the wrong locus, or fail to align. This can reduce the apparent expression of the non-reference allele [CITE Degner 2009, Stevenson 2013]. This measurement artefact is known as reference-genome mapping bias or reference allele bias."),
(KEEP,"The FVB inbred strain is widely used in mouse models of cancer, metabolic disease, and cardiovascular physiology. It includes the FVB/NJ substrain maintained by the Jackson Laboratory and FVB/N substrains derived from the NIH colony. FVB/N has also been widely used for transgenic model generation because of its favourable reproductive performance and prominent pronuclei [CITE Taketo 1991]. The MMTV-PyVT mammary tumour model, one of the most widely used mouse cancer models, was also established on the FVB/N background [CITE Guy 1992]."),
("R1-M2","FVB is therefore a common background for RNA-seq studies, but this creates a practical concern. In studies comparing FVB and B6 mice, SNP-mediated mapping bias may affect expression estimates at variant-rich loci. The same issue may arise when FVB-background transgenic mice are compared with wild-type controls. Despite this concern, the scale of reference-genome mapping bias in FVB RNA-seq has not been well defined. In our survey of publicly available GEO series containing FVB RNA-seq data, all studies that reported a reference genome used a B6-based assembly, and none reported a strain-aware alignment strategy, even though a FVB/NJ genome assembly has been available for more than a decade [CITE Wong 2012]."),
(KEEP,"Two main approaches are available to reduce SNP-mediated reference-genome mapping bias. The first is WASP, or Variant-Aware Software for Polymorphism, which identifies reads whose alignment changes after allele substitution at variant sites and removes them [CITE van de Geijn 2015]. WASP can be applied to standard reference-genome alignments and does not require a personalised reference genome. However, it was developed mainly for allele-specific expression and molecular QTL analyses in heterozygous samples, and its practical effect in pure-strain FVB versus B6 comparisons remains unclear."),
(KEEP,"A second approach is personalised reference alignment, in which strain-specific variant calls are incorporated into the reference sequence before alignment so that the template better matches the strain being analysed. This strategy can improve read recovery at variant-containing loci [CITE Munger 2014], but it requires reference construction, annotation handling, and careful interpretation of coordinate systems. Personalised genomes have therefore been used more often in genetic reference populations, such as Diversity Outbred mice, than in routine pure-strain FVB RNA-seq studies [CITE Churchill 2012]."),
(KEEP,"The magnitude and genomic distribution of FVB reference-genome mapping bias remain uncertain. Without this information, it is difficult to decide when correction is needed or which genes are most likely to be affected. Tissue context may further influence the observed bias because different tissues express different transcripts, cover different SNPs, and have different read-depth distributions. The set of measurably biased genes may therefore vary across datasets."),
("CORR","In this study, we systematically characterised SNP-mediated reference-genome mapping bias in FVB RNA-seq. We first used F1 hybrid mice from two independent FVB × B6 crosses to measure allele-level reference bias directly. We then processed FVB/NJ versus C57BL/6J RNA-seq data from four tissues of two laboratories («n_animals_strain_comparison_FVB|d» FVB/NJ and «n_animals_strain_comparison_B6|d» C57BL/6J animals), with a B6-congenic tumour dataset as a negative control, through three alignment arms: naive B6 alignment, WASP-filtered B6 alignment, and FVB/NJ personalised reference alignment. Because the whole comparison rests on the strain of each public sample being what its record says, we verified the strain of every sample by sequence before use. This framework allowed us to assess the genome-wide impact of reference choice and identify reproducibly biased genes. Our aim was not to re-interpret the biology of individual public datasets, but to provide a reusable analytical framework and a locus-level warning catalog for FVB RNA-seq studies."),
])
