K1_RESULTS=("Under unique-only counting the FVB/NJ reads at this locus are not recovered by the SNP-patched reference, and the bias is essentially unchanged by multi-mapping inclusion (residual fraction «ex_extreme_skin_residual|.2f» in skin; «ex_extreme_residual_min|.2f»–«ex_extreme_residual_max|.2f» per tissue). "
 "The reads are not absent: they are multi-mappers on both references. On the B6 reference they are assigned predominantly to two processed pseudogenes of *Rps2* (fractional counts «rps2mm_Rps2_ps10_naive_multi_FVB_cpm|.1f» and «rps2mm_Rps2_ps13_naive_multi_FVB_cpm|.1f» counts per million in FVB/NJ skin against «rps2_skin_naive_multi_FVB_cpm|.1f» at *Rps2*); on the SNP-patched FVB/NJ reference they are divided almost equally among *Rps2* and the same two pseudogenes («rps2_skin_fvb_multi_FVB_cpm|.1f», «rps2mm_Rps2_ps10_fvb_multi_FVB_cpm|.1f» and «rps2mm_Rps2_ps13_fvb_multi_FVB_cpm|.1f»); unique assignment to the pseudogenes is negligible in both strains (repository files `rps2_indels_pseudogenes.md` and `rps2_multimap_cpm.tsv`). "
 "The SNP-patched *Rps2* therefore matches the FVB/NJ reads as well as the pseudogenes do, but not better, so the reads remain multi-mappers and are excluded from unique-only counts. "
 "Because fractional counting raises the FVB/NJ-to-C57BL/6J ratio of *Rps2* by a similar amount on both references (by «rps2_skin_naive_log2ratio_shift|.2f» and «rps2_skin_fvb_log2ratio_shift|.2f» log₂ units), the bias is unchanged when multi-mapping reads are included. "
 "Whether the indels of the FVB/NJ allele would separate *Rps2* from its pseudogenes cannot be tested with a SNP-only reference: FVB/NJ carries «rps2_indels_gene_body|d» homozygous indels in the *Rps2* gene body, four of them within exons (including a 12-bp insertion), and none is in the reference because the Mouse Genomes Project release carries indels without a PASS filter and g2gtools therefore omitted them (Discussion).")
# ============================================================ RESULTS (J-3) ==================================================
res=[("H","# Results"),
("H","## Publicly available FVB RNA-seq studies overwhelmingly use B6 reference genomes without strain-aware correction"),
(KEEP,"We first examined how publicly available FVB RNA-seq datasets had been processed. We retrieved data-processing metadata from «survey_total|d» GEO series that generated RNA-seq data from FVB mice. These included studies reporting FVB/NJ, FVB/N, or simply \"FVB\" as the strain."),
("REF","Among these «survey_total|d» series, «survey_stated_B6_n|d» reported the reference genome used for alignment. All «survey_stated_B6_n|d» used a C57BL/6J-derived reference assembly, either GRCm38/mm10 or GRCm39/mm39. The remaining «survey_not_reported_n|d» studies did not report a reference genome. No study reported a strain-aware alignment strategy in the public data-processing metadata, and we found no mention of WASP, g2gtools, personalised genome construction, or N-masking [CITE Krueger 2016] (Fig. 1A). Because classification was based on public metadata, these results should be interpreted as a reporting and reproducibility finding rather than as proof that no unpublished local correction was performed. This B6-derived category also included legacy mm9-based analyses, as shown in Supplementary Table S5; the survey should therefore be read as covering standard C57BL/6-derived mouse assemblies rather than only GRCm38/GRCm39."),
("R1-m4","Together, these results indicate that most FVB RNA-seq studies use a B6 reference genome and that strain-aware correction is rarely reported, despite the genetic divergence between FVB and B6 (Fig. 1B)."),
("H","## B6-reference alignment underestimates FVB allele representation in F1 hybrid mice"),
("CORR","We next tested whether B6-reference alignment produces allele-level bias. For this analysis, we used F1 hybrid mice from two independent datasets. GSE293440 contained frontal cortex samples from B6 × FVB F1 mice (GEO strain attribute \"B6xFVB\"; the FVB substrain is not stated). GSE200632 contained primary keratinocyte samples from C57BL/6 × FVB/N F1 mice. The F3 backcross samples of GSE200632 were excluded. All 12 animals were confirmed as F1 hybrids by the sequence-based identity check described below."),
("NEW-F1","In F1 mice, each autosomal locus carries one allele from each parental strain. Therefore, the expected FVB allele fraction at heterozygous sites is 0.50 under unbiased mapping. We measured allele-specific read counts at the «f1_site_set_n|,d» exonic sites at which FVB/NJ is homozygous for the alternative allele, at every one of which an F1 animal is expected to be heterozygous (the site set of the identity check described below)."),
("NEW-F1","Under naive B6-reference alignment, the mean FVB allele fraction across all 12 F1 samples was «f1_naive_pooled_mean|.3f» ± «f1_naive_pooled_se|.4f». This was below the expected value of 0.50. The difference was significant by one-sample t-test (t = «f1_naive_t|.1f», «f1_naive_p|p»; Fig. 2A). This corresponds to a «f1_naive_deficit_pp|.1f»-percentage-point deficit in FVB allele representation."),
("NEW-F1","The same pattern was seen in both datasets. The mean FVB allele fraction was «f1_naive_GSE200632_mean|.3f» in GSE200632 and «f1_naive_GSE293440_mean|.3f» in GSE293440. Each sample contributed «f1_naive_sites_min|,d»–«f1_naive_sites_max|,d» heterozygous sites after filtering. The mean number of sites per sample was «f1_naive_sites_mean|,d». Sites were retained when total depth was at least 20 and the minor allele count was at least 3. The GSE200632 estimate should be interpreted cautiously because the cross involved FVB/N rather than FVB/NJ, but the direction of bias was concordant with the GSE293440 dataset."),
("NEW-F1","This consistent shift was observed across two independent F1 datasets generated from different crosses and tissues: GSE200632 from C57BL/6 × FVB/N keratinocytes and GSE293440 from B6 × FVB frontal cortex. Genuine allele-specific expression (ASE), including imprinting or cis-regulatory effects, may occur at individual loci, but such biological ASE would not be expected to produce a uniform genome-wide underrepresentation of the FVB allele across independent tissues and crosses. The FVB allele fraction was consistently below the expected value of 0.50 in both datasets, with mean fractions of «f1_naive_GSE200632_mean|.3f» and «f1_naive_GSE293440_mean|.3f», respectively, and «f1_naive_pooled_mean|.3f» overall. The reproducible direction of imbalance across distinct biological contexts supports systematic reference-mapping bias as the dominant explanation."),
("NEW-F1","WASP filtering did not restore the allele balance but overcorrected it. The mean FVB allele fraction increased to «f1_wasp_pooled_mean|.3f» ± «f1_wasp_pooled_se|.4f». It was significantly above 0.50 (t = «f1_wasp_t|.1f», «f1_wasp_p|p»; Fig. 2A). Site-level distributions showed the same leftward shift under naive alignment and a rightward shift after WASP filtering (Fig. 2B, C). The site-level comparison shows why: at the evaluated sites WASP removed «f1_wasp_ref_removed_pct_min|.0f»–«f1_wasp_ref_removed_pct_max|.0f»% of the reads carrying the B6 (reference) allele but only «f1_wasp_alt_removed_pct_min|.0f»–«f1_wasp_alt_removed_pct_max|.0f»% of the reads carrying the FVB allele, so the FVB fraction rose at «f1_wasp_sites_af_increased_pct_min|.0f»–«f1_wasp_sites_af_increased_pct_max|.0f»% of sites and fell at fewer than «f1_wasp_sites_af_decreased_pct_max|.1f»% (repository file `f1_wasp_allele_removal.tsv`). WASP discards a read if its allele-swapped copy no longer maps to the same place. A read from the B6 haplotype aligns to the B6 reference without mismatches, but its swapped copy carries the FVB alleles of every variant it covers — with 150-bp paired-end reads, often several SNPs in a SNP-dense exon — and fails to remap; a read from the FVB haplotype that did align already carried those mismatches, and its swapped copy maps perfectly. The FVB-haplotype reads that failed to align in the naive arm, which are the cause of the deficit, are not in the alignment at all and cannot be recovered by filtering."),
("NEW-F1/PHASE-K","The deficit is concentrated where the pure-strain bias is: at the evaluated sites that lie inside the «catalog_n|d» catalog genes described below («f1_catalog_sites_naive_n_min|d»–«f1_catalog_sites_naive_n_max|d» per animal), the naive FVB allele fraction was «f1_catalog_sites_naive_mean_af|.3f», against «f1_other_sites_naive_mean_af|.3f» at the other sites (paired t-test over the 12 animals, «f1_catalog_vs_other_naive_p|p»; Fig. 2D), a deficit of «f1_catalog_sites_naive_deficit_pp|.0f» percentage points. Under WASP the two values were «f1_catalog_sites_wasp_mean_af|.3f» and «f1_other_sites_wasp_mean_af|.3f» (p = «f1_catalog_vs_other_wasp_p|.3f»)."),
("NEW-F1","These results indicate that B6-reference alignment systematically suppresses apparent FVB allele representation, by a small amount genome-wide and by a large amount at SNP-dense loci, and that read-level filtering moves the estimate past the expected FVB allele fraction rather than on to it."),
("H","## Every sample entering the pure-strain comparison was verified by sequence"),
]
for p in v2sec("results.md","Every sample entering the pure-strain comparison was verified by sequence"): res.append(("NEW-ID",p))
res+=[
("H","## FVB/NJ-personalised reference alignment reduces locus-specific bias without shifting the genome-wide distribution"),
("CORR","We then examined whether allele-level bias affects gene-level expression estimates in pure-strain comparisons. FVB/NJ versus C57BL/6J RNA-seq data from four tissues of two laboratories were processed through three alignment arms (Table 1). These were naive B6 alignment, WASP-filtered B6 alignment, and FVB/NJ personalised reference alignment. The strain contrast was estimated with edgeR in a model that included diet (fructose study) or treatment and sex (imiquimod study) as covariates."),
(KEEP,"For each gene, we defined reference bias as the difference between the FVB-reference log₂ fold change and the naive B6-reference log₂ fold change:"),
(KEEP,"bias = log₂FC_FVB ref − log₂FC_naive"),
(KEEP,"A positive value means that naive B6 alignment underestimates FVB expression relative to B6."),
("R1-m5","Genes with positive bias were visible as points above the diagonal in per-gene scatter plots. The liver dataset is shown as a representative example (Fig. 3A). Most genes remained close to the diagonal. A small number of genes showed clear upward displacement, indicating higher FVB/B6 log₂FC after FVB-reference alignment."),
("CORR","Across the four tissues, «genes_tested_union|,d» genes were expressed and quantifiable in at least one tissue. The genome-wide median bias was within ±«gw_median_bias_absmax|.3f» log₂ units of zero in every tissue (Fig. 3B). The mean bias was also close to zero, with values ranging from «gw_mean_bias_min|.3f» to «gw_mean_bias_max|.3f» log₂ units."),
(KEEP,"Thus, the FVB/NJ personalised reference did not create a global upward shift in FVB expression; the effect was restricted to a small subset of genes."),
("CORR","The proportion of genes with bias ≥ 0.5 log₂ units was small in every tissue. It ranged from «gw_pct_ge_0.5_min|.2f»% in hypothalamus to «gw_pct_ge_0.5_max|.2f»% in adipose tissue. Across all four tissues, «genes_ge_0.5_any_tissue|d» unique genes showed bias ≥ 0.5 in at least one tissue. This represented «genes_ge_0.5_any_tissue_pct|.2f»% of the «genes_tested_union|,d» expressed genes."),
(KEEP,"Dataset-level mean log₂FC values were stable across the three alignment arms (Fig. 3C), indicating that reference-genome bias is concentrated at a limited number of loci rather than distributed across the transcriptome."),
("H","## WASP filtering has little genome-wide effect in pure-strain comparisons"),
(KEEP,"We next examined the WASP-filtered arm in the pure-strain datasets. The WASP arm was generated by retaining reads with no vW tag and reads with vW=1. Reads with a vW tag other than 1 were removed. This keeps reads that do not overlap FVB/NJ SNPs and reads that pass the WASP re-mapping test."),
("CORR","With this filter, WASP removed only a small fraction of assigned reads. Across all tissues and strains, the removed fraction ranged from «wasp_removed_min_pct|.2f»% to «wasp_removed_max_pct|.2f»% (Supplementary Table S4). This is consistent with the low median SNP density across expressed genes. The median genome-wide FVB/NJ SNP density was «pred_median_snp_density_all_tested|.2f» SNPs/kb."),
("CORR","C57BL/6J samples lost more reads than FVB/NJ samples in every tissue (C57BL/6J «wasp_removed_B6_min_pct|.2f»–«wasp_removed_B6_max_pct|.2f»% versus FVB/NJ «wasp_removed_FVB_min_pct|.2f»–«wasp_removed_FVB_max_pct|.2f»%). The FVB-specific attrition relative to B6 ranged from «wasp_removed_FVB_minus_B6_pp_min|.2f» to «wasp_removed_FVB_minus_B6_pp_max|.2f» percentage points. This pattern suggests that B6 reads overlapping FVB variant positions can also be affected by allele-swap re-mapping."),
("CORR","The effect of WASP on gene-level fold changes was minimal. The genome-wide mean log₂FC under WASP alignment differed from naive alignment by at most «gw_wasp_minus_naive_absmax|.3f» log₂ units. The changes were «gw_adipose_wasp_minus_naive_mean_logFC|+.3f» in adipose tissue, «gw_hypothalamus_wasp_minus_naive_mean_logFC|+.3f» in hypothalamus, «gw_liver_wasp_minus_naive_mean_logFC|+.3f» in liver, and «gw_skin_wasp_minus_naive_mean_logFC|+.3f» in skin (Fig. 3C)."),
(KEEP,"Thus, WASP filtering removed a small set of reference-sensitive reads but had little genome-wide impact in pure-strain FVB versus B6 comparisons, probably because most reads did not overlap FVB/NJ SNP sites."),
# Phase P-4: paragraph "This near-zero genome-wide effect should not be interpreted …" removed (explained once, in the Discussion)
("H","## SNP-dense loci are enriched among genes vulnerable to reference-genome bias"),
(KEEP,"We next asked which gene features were associated with reference-genome bias. We evaluated four predictors: FVB/NJ SNP density, gene length, gene biotype, and mean expression level."),
("CORR","Across all expressed genes, the correlation between SNP density and mean bias was moderate and significant (Spearman ρ = «pred_snp_density_per_kb_rho|.3f», «pred_snp_density_per_kb_p|p»). The correlation was stronger among the «pred_n_all4tissue_genes|,d» genes tested in all four tissues, for which the mean bias is least noisy (ρ = «pred_snp_rho_all4tissue_genes|.3f»), and SNP-dense genes were strongly enriched among the biased loci."),
("CORR","Genes in the top 10% of SNP density had higher mean bias than genes in the bottom 10% (Mann–Whitney U test, «pred_top_vs_bottom_decile_bias_MWU_p|p»). Catalog genes were especially enriched for high SNP density (Fig. 4A). Their median SNP density was «pred_catalog_median_snp_density|.2f» SNPs/kb, compared with «pred_noncatalog_median_snp_density|.2f» SNPs/kb for non-catalog expressed genes."),
("CORR","Gene length also showed a weak but significant correlation with bias (Spearman ρ = «pred_log10_length_rho|.3f», «pred_log10_length_p|p»; Fig. 4B). This likely reflects the fact that longer genes contain more variants in absolute terms. Mean expression level was weakly negatively correlated with bias (Spearman ρ = «pred_mean_logCPM_naive_rho|.3f», «pred_mean_logCPM_naive_p|p»; Fig. 4C), and the binned mean bias stayed within ±«pred_expression_binned_mean_absmax|.2f» log₂ units across the expression range. This suggests that the bias is not simply a low-expression artifact."),
("CORR","Gene biotype showed only modest differences in bias distribution (Fig. 4D). Protein-coding genes and lncRNAs had mean bias values close to zero. Pseudogenes showed a more negative mean bias, but the median bias was within ±«pred_biotype_median_absmax|.3f» of zero across all biotype categories (Kruskal–Wallis «pred_biotype_kruskal_p|p»)."),
(KEEP,"Among the predictors tested, SNP density had the clearest biological interpretation: genes with many FVB/NJ SNPs are more likely to contain reads that are penalized during B6-reference alignment."),
("H","## A catalog of «catalog_n|d» genes consistently biased across non-tumour tissues"),
("CORR","We then constructed a catalog of reproducibly biased genes. A gene was included if it showed bias ≥ 0.5 log₂ units in at least two of the four non-tumour tissues. These tissues were adipose tissue, hypothalamus, liver, and skin. The B6-congenic PyVT mammary tumour dataset provides no FVB-versus-B6 contrast and was used as a negative control (below)."),
("CORR","This criterion identified «catalog_n|d» genes (Fig. 5A; Supplementary Table S2), representing «catalog_pct_of_tested|.2f»% of the «genes_tested_union|,d» expressed genes. The catalog was essentially unchanged when the strain contrast was estimated from the control-condition animals alone (water-fed or control-cream animals; «catalog_water_n|d» genes, «catalog_water_shared|d» shared with the primary catalog; Supplementary Table S3), showing that the diet and treatment covariates do not drive it."),
("CORR","The catalog genes varied in tissue breadth. «catalog_n_tissues_4|w» genes met the bias threshold in all four non-tumour tissues. «catalog_n_tissues_3|w» genes met the threshold in three tissues. «catalog_n_tissues_2|w» genes met the threshold in two tissues (Fig. 5B). Because the criterion is applied per tissue, «catalog_mean_bias_lt_0.5|d» catalog genes have a mean bias over their tested tissues below 0.5 log₂."),
("CORR","Most catalog genes were protein-coding. This category accounted for «catalog_biotype_protein_coding|d» of the «catalog_n|d» genes. The remaining genes included «catalog_biotype_lncRNA|d» lncRNAs, «catalog_biotype_pseudogene|d» pseudogenes, and «catalog_biotype_other|d» genes of other biotypes."),
("CORR","Catalog genes were strongly enriched for FVB/NJ SNP density. Their median SNP density was «pred_catalog_median_snp_density|.2f» SNPs/kb, compared with «pred_noncatalog_median_snp_density|.2f» SNPs/kb for non-catalog expressed genes (Mann–Whitney U test, «pred_catalog_vs_noncatalog_snp_density_MWU_p|p»; Fig. 4A). Genes in the top 10% of SNP density were «pred_top_decile_OR|.0f»-fold enriched among catalog genes (odds ratio = «pred_top_decile_OR|.1f», 95% confidence interval = «pred_top_decile_OR_ci_lo|.1f»–«pred_top_decile_OR_ci_hi|.1f», «pred_top_decile_fisher_p|p»)."),
("R1-M1/PHASE-P","The catalog genes are not scattered at random. «catalog_cluster_genes_n|d» of the «catalog_n|d» lie in «catalog_cluster_n|d» clusters of at least three catalog genes (catalog genes on the same chromosome with gene starts less than 1 Mb apart; Supplementary Table S2, column cluster), most of them multigene families: the MHC on chromosome 17 («catalog_cluster_mhc_genes|d» genes in «catalog_cluster_mhc_clusters|wl» clusters), the replication-dependent histone genes on chromosome 13 («catalog_cluster_hist_genes|d» genes in «catalog_cluster_hist_clusters|wl» clusters), the major urinary protein cluster on chromosome 4 («catalog_cluster_mup_genes|d» genes) and the Ifi200 cluster on chromosome 1 («catalog_cluster_ifi_genes|d» genes). «catalog_cluster_genes_multi_n|d» of these «catalog_cluster_genes_n|d» genes are in the SNP-induced multi-mapping class. These are regions in which strains differ in haplotype and gene structure, not only at single nucleotides, and in which paralogous copies compete for the same reads."),
("NEW-WE","*Ndufs5* was the most consistently biased gene. This protein-coding gene on chromosome «ex_robust_chrom|s» contained «ex_robust_n_snps|d» FVB/NJ SNPs across a «ex_robust_length_kb|.1f»-kb gene body and exceeded the bias threshold in all four non-tumour tissues. Bias values were «ex_robust_adipose_bias|+.2f» in adipose tissue, «ex_robust_hypothalamus_bias|+.2f» in hypothalamus, «ex_robust_liver_bias|+.2f» in liver, and «ex_robust_skin_bias|+.2f» in skin, with a four-tissue mean bias of «ex_robust_mean_bias|+.2f»."),
("NEW-WE","The «catalog_n_tissues_4|d» four-tissue genes included several ribosomal protein genes (*Rps2*, *Rps18*, *Rpl34*, *Rpl3*, *Rpl35a*), *Atp5g2*, *Acp1* and *Carnmt1*, and the MHC-related *H2-Q5* qualifying in three tissues."),
("CORR","Some genes showed very large bias in fewer tissues. *Gm25939* showed strong bias in adipose tissue, hypothalamus, and liver. The values were «catalog_gene_Gm25939_adipose_bias|+.2f», «catalog_gene_Gm25939_hypothalamus_bias|+.2f», and «catalog_gene_Gm25939_liver_bias|+.2f» log₂ units, respectively. It was not detected in skin."),
("CORR","The number of catalog genes differed by tissue. Adipose tissue contained «catalog_biased_in_adipose|d» of the «catalog_n|d» genes. Liver contained «catalog_biased_in_liver|d». Hypothalamus contained «catalog_biased_in_hypothalamus|d». Skin contained «catalog_biased_in_skin|d»."),
(KEEP,"The catalog was also examined under related threshold settings (Supplementary Table S3)."),
("H","## The bias is a property of FVB/NJ reads, not of the loci: a B6-background negative control"),
]
for p in v2sec("results.md","The bias is a property of FVB/NJ reads, not of the loci: a B6-background negative control"): res.append(("NEW-NC",p))
res+=[
("H","## Multi-mapping sensitivity analysis reveals two mechanistic classes"),
(KEEP,"The primary analysis used the default featureCounts setting, which excludes multi-mapping reads. We therefore tested whether catalog-gene bias depended on multi-mapper exclusion. For this sensitivity analysis, we used featureCounts with -M --fraction."),
("CORR","Across «mm_n_pairs|,d» expressed gene-tissue pairs from the four non-tumour tissues, multi-mapping inclusion had little effect on the overall bias distribution. The median change in bias was «mm_median_delta|+.3f» log₂ units. The mean change was «mm_mean_delta|+.3f». Only «mm_pct_abs_gt_0.5|.1f»% of gene-tissue pairs showed an absolute change greater than 0.5 log₂ units (Supplementary Fig. S2A)."),
("CORR","The effect was larger among catalog genes. «mech_multi_n|w» of the «catalog_n|d» catalog genes showed |Δ| > 0.3 log₂ units, where Δ was defined as bias_multi − bias_unique. These genes were classified as the SNP-induced multi-mapping class. For these genes, including multi-mapping reads partially restored FVB signal in the naive arm and reduced the measured bias. The mean |Δ| in this class was «mech_multi_mean_absdelta|.2f» log₂ units (Supplementary Fig. S2B)."),
("CORR","«mech_multi_residual_le_0.30|w» of these «mech_multi_n|d» genes had residual fractions at or below 0.30. This means that more than 70% of the unique-only bias was reduced after multi-mapping reads were included. This pattern is consistent with FVB reads becoming multi-mappers under B6-reference alignment (Supplementary Fig. S2C)."),
("CORR/PHASE-K1","The remaining «mech_direct_n|d» catalog genes showed |Δ| ≤ 0.3 log₂ units. These genes were classified as the direct SNP misalignment class. Their bias persisted after multi-mapping reads were included, consistent with the causative reads failing to align, being assigned to another locus, or remaining multi-mappers on both references. The mean |Δ| in this class was «mech_direct_mean_absdelta|.2f» log₂ units."),
("CORR/PHASE-K/PHASE-K1","Several genes showed a bias that was essentially unchanged by multi-mapping inclusion. These included *Rps2*, *Rpl29* and *Klra1*. *Rps2* had a residual fraction of «catalog_gene_Rps2_residual|.2f» and the largest single-tissue bias in this class («ex_extreme_skin_bias|+.2f» log₂, skin). *Rpl29* had a residual fraction of «catalog_gene_Rpl29_residual|.2f». *Klra1* had a residual fraction of «catalog_gene_Klra1_residual|.2f». Representative genes from both mechanistic classes are shown by tissue in Supplementary Fig. S2D."),
(KEEP,"Per-gene Δ values, residual fractions, and mechanism classes are provided in Supplementary Table S2 and Supplementary Note 2."),
(KEEP,"Under standard pipelines, both mechanisms have the same practical consequence: FVB expression is underestimated at the affected locus. The catalog bias values therefore represent the bias that investigators would observe when using default unique-only featureCounts settings."),
("H","## Decomposition of the bias into FVB/NJ-read gain and C57BL/6J-read loss"),
]
for p in v2sec("results.md","Decomposition of the bias into FVB/NJ-read gain and C57BL/6J-read loss"): res.append(("NEW-DEC",p))
res.append(("H","## *Ndufs5* and *Rps2* as worked examples of the two mechanism classes"))
for p in v2sec("results.md","*Ndufs5* and *Rps2* as worked examples of the two mechanisms"):
    a="In adipose, hypothalamus and liver, where the reads are longer and paired, *Rps2* qualifies under both bias definitions (bias adipose +1.12, hypothalamus +1.36, liver +1.14, log₂; bias_matched +0.73, +1.06, +0.86 log₂; Fig. 6D) and part of the FVB/NJ signal is recovered. "
    b="Both worked-example genes lie among the SNP-dense, positively biased loci relative to the genome-wide background (Fig. 6C). "
    if a+b in p: p=p.replace(a+b, b+a)   # Fig. 6C must be cited before Fig. 6D (R1-m5 rule)
    trig="NEW-WE"
    k0=p.find("The FVB/NJ reads at this locus are not recovered by the SNP-patched reference")
    if k0>=0:   # Phase K-1'(b): Rps2 read fate rewritten from rps2_multimap_cpm.tsv (authors' wording, 2026-10-22)
        k1=p.index("(repository file `rps2_indels_pseudogenes.md`).", k0)+len("(repository file `rps2_indels_pseudogenes.md`).")
        p=p[:k0]+K1_RESULTS+p[k1:]; trig="NEW-WE/PHASE-K1"
    res.append((trig,p))
