# ============================================================ DISCUSSION (J-4) ===============================================
disc=[("H","# Discussion"),
("CORR","This study shows that SNP-mediated reference-genome mapping bias in FVB RNA-seq is reproducible, interpretable, and limited in scope. Every pure-strain animal in this analysis is a Jackson Laboratory FVB/NJ animal, so the catalog is substrain-matched throughout. At the same time, the bias was not distributed across the transcriptome. The «catalog_n|d» genes in our catalog accounted for only «catalog_pct_of_tested|.2f»% of the «genes_tested_union|,d» expressed genes examined across four tissues from two laboratories. The F1 hybrids show the same picture at the allele level: across all evaluated variant sites the FVB allele deficit under naive alignment is only «f1_naive_deficit_pp|.1f» percentage points, whereas at the variant sites inside the «catalog_n|d» catalog genes the same animals show a «f1_catalog_sites_naive_deficit_pp|.0f»-point deficit. The small genome-wide deficit in the F1 animals and the concentration of the pure-strain bias at a few loci are two views of the same phenomenon. Previous FVB versus B6 RNA-seq studies are therefore unlikely to be broadly invalidated by reference-genome choice alone, and pathway-level or genome-wide conclusions are probably robust in many cases. The main concern is narrower: a small number of SNP-dense loci can be substantially underestimated when FVB reads are aligned to a B6 reference."),
("REF","We therefore view reference choice as a reproducibility-relevant technical variable, not as a reason to dismiss existing FVB RNA-seq datasets. It should be reported and checked when interpreting gene-level results, especially when a gene of interest lies in a high-SNP-density region or appears in the catalog generated here. In mouse experiments, strain background is part of the experimental system. Substrain, vendor, sex, age, housing, and other biological or environmental variables can all influence experimental outcomes [CITE Mekada 2009, Percie du Sert 2020]. Reference genome choice should be considered alongside these variables because it can be easy to overlook but consequential at specific loci."),
(KEEP,"This finding is also relevant to the 3Rs in animal research, particularly Reduction. Public RNA-seq datasets contain information obtained from experimental animals. If strain-dependent mapping bias is overlooked, investigators may misinterpret gene-level differences and pursue unnecessary follow-up experiments. A strain-aware reanalysis framework can help extract more reliable information from existing animal-derived datasets before new animal experiments are planned. The present catalog should therefore be viewed as a practical triage tool for avoiding artefact-driven follow-up work rather than as a replacement for biological validation."),
(KEEP,"The contribution to Refinement is more indirect, but still relevant. Refinement is often discussed in relation to procedures that reduce pain, distress, or procedural burden. In this study, the refinement is analytical rather than procedural: a more appropriate reference-genome strategy improves the interpretation of data already obtained from animals and helps ensure that each animal-derived dataset yields reliable information. Reporting the reference genome and applying strain-aware checks at vulnerable loci should therefore be considered part of good analytical practice for FVB RNA-seq studies."),
("CORR","FVB mice are a particularly relevant setting for this issue. FVB/NJ and FVB/N are widely used in transgenic and disease-model research. FVB/N has been used extensively for transgenic mouse production because of its favourable reproductive performance and prominent pronuclei [CITE Taketo 1991]. The MMTV-PyVT mammary tumour model was also established on the FVB/N background [CITE Guy 1992]. In practice, FVB/NJ and FVB/N are often treated as a single FVB background, although the substrains are not necessarily identical. The personalised reference used in this study was built from FVB/NJ variant calls from the Mouse Genomes Project [CITE Keane 2011, Lilue 2018]. FVB/N has accumulated substrain-specific genetic drift relative to FVB/NJ since the two colonies were separated [CITE Taketo 1991], and FVB/N-private variants are not captured by the FVB/NJ VCI; investigators using FVB/N mice, or FVB-background transgenic lines maintained separately for many generations, should expect the catalog to be largely but not exactly applicable. Investigators using FVB/N mice would nevertheless ideally use a FVB/N-specific variant set for the most precise personalised reference. For the F1 ASE analysis, GSE200632 (keratinocytes, C57BL/6 × FVB/N) is not substrain-matched to the VCI and the FVB substrain of the GSE293440 cross is not recorded in GEO; the ASE values of both datasets reflect bias at FVB/NJ-defined sites only, which is sufficient for the purpose of that analysis."),
("NEW-WE","*Ndufs5* provides the clearest example. It exceeded the bias threshold in all four tissues and in both laboratories, it meets the catalog criterion under both definitions of the bias, and most of its FVB/NJ signal returns to the naive arm when multi-mapping reads are included (Supplementary Note 2). *Ndufs5* is therefore the cleanest example of a reproducibly biased FVB locus in this study."),
("NEW-WE/PHASE-K/PHASE-K1","*Rps2* illustrates a different pattern and marks the limit of a SNP-only personalised reference. It showed the largest single-tissue bias of the direct-misalignment class, in skin, where the apparent «ex_extreme_naive_fold|.0f»-fold difference between the strains disappears under the personalised reference through loss of C57BL/6J reads, not recovery of FVB/NJ reads under unique-only counting. The true expression of *Rps2* in FVB/NJ skin therefore remains undetermined; resolving it requires a personalised reference that includes the indel set, or quantification against the FVB_NJ_v3 assembly."),
("R2-C3","A key result is the contrast between the F1 and pure-strain analyses. In F1 hybrids, naive B6-reference alignment reduced the apparent FVB allele fraction, consistent with previous work showing that reference-genome alignment can bias allele-specific read counts toward the reference allele [CITE Degner 2009, Stevenson 2013]. WASP overcorrected this imbalance: it removes reads whose alignment changes after allele substitution [CITE van de Geijn 2015], which at a heterozygous site discards B6-allele reads («f1_wasp_ref_removed_pct_min|.0f»–«f1_wasp_ref_removed_pct_max|.0f»% of them in the F1 animals) far more often than FVB-allele reads («f1_wasp_alt_removed_pct_min|.0f»–«f1_wasp_alt_removed_pct_max|.0f»%), because the allele-swapped copy of a B6-haplotype read acquires the FVB mismatches of every SNP it covers whereas the swapped copy of an FVB-haplotype read loses them; the FVB-haplotype reads that never aligned, which are the cause of the deficit, cannot be recovered by any filter. In an F1 sample, both parental alleles are present in the same biological background, allowing allele-specific mapping bias to be measured directly. The F1 analyses do not exclude genuine ASE at individual genes. The key point is that aggregate FVB allele underrepresentation was reproduced across independent F1 datasets from different tissues and crosses, a pattern more consistent with systematic mapping bias than with tissue-specific imprinting or cis-regulatory ASE. The same logic did not produce a large genome-wide effect in pure-strain comparisons. In these datasets, WASP removed only «wasp_removed_min_pct|.2f»–«wasp_removed_max_pct|.2f»% of assigned reads and changed genome-wide mean log₂ fold changes by no more than «gw_wasp_minus_naive_absmax|.3f» log₂ units. This does not indicate a failure of the filter; rather, most RNA-seq reads do not overlap FVB/NJ SNP sites. WASP can remove reads that fail the allele-swap re-mapping test, but it cannot recover FVB reads that were lost or misassigned during the initial B6-reference alignment [CITE van de Geijn 2015]. For pure-strain FVB versus B6 comparisons, a strain-matched reference is therefore more useful than read-level filtering alone. For investigators focused on gene-level differential expression between pure strains, personalised reference alignment is the more effective correction strategy for the small number of vulnerable loci."),
("NEW-WE/PHASE-K1","The personalised reference used here was deliberately conservative. It was constructed by substituting FVB/NJ SNPs into the GRCm39 B6 reference using g2gtools. Because the MGP REL-2021 indel VCF used FILTER=\".\" rather than FILTER=\"PASS\" for indel records, indels were excluded by the g2gtools --pass step. As a result, the personalised reference was SNP-only. This has two consequences. First, coordinates remained compatible with the B6 annotation, which simplified the three-arm comparison. Second, our correction does not address indel- or structural-variant-mediated mapping bias. Residual bias may therefore remain at loci where indels or larger variants affect read alignment. The catalog should be interpreted as a conservative catalog of SNP-mediated reference bias, not as a complete map of all strain-dependent mappability effects. This SNP-only implementation strengthens the specificity of the catalog for SNP-mediated bias, but it may underestimate total strain-dependent bias at loci affected by indels, structural variants, or FVB/N-private variants. The *Rps2* skin locus is the clearest case: the SNP-only reference recovers no FVB/NJ reads there under unique-only counting (bias_matched «decomp_Rps2_skin_bias_matched|+.2f» log₂; Fig. 6B), so the correction is incomplete and the true FVB/NJ expression remains undetermined."),
("CORR","The enrichment of catalog genes for FVB/NJ SNP density supports this interpretation. The median SNP density of catalog genes was «pred_catalog_median_snp_density|.2f» SNPs/kb, compared with «pred_noncatalog_median_snp_density|.2f» SNPs/kb in background expressed genes. SNP density alone did not predict bias with high accuracy; the genome-wide correlation was moderate (ρ = «pred_snp_density_per_kb_rho|.3f»; «pred_snp_rho_all4tissue_genes|.3f» in genes tested in all four tissues) because most genes, even those with some SNPs, showed little measurable bias. Nevertheless, high SNP density was clearly overrepresented among the affected loci. In practice, SNP density is a useful first-pass warning signal, and genes with many FVB-specific SNPs warrant extra caution when analysed with a B6 reference."),
("CORR","The multi-mapping sensitivity analysis adds a mechanistic layer to this interpretation. Catalog genes fell into two broad classes. In one class, FVB reads appear to become multi-mappers under B6-reference alignment and are then excluded by the standard unique-only featureCounts pipeline [CITE Liao 2014]. Including multi-mapping reads with fractional assignment reduced the apparent bias at these genes. *Ndufs5*, the ribosomal protein genes *Rps18*, *Rpl34*, *Rpl3* and *Rpl35a*, and the major urinary protein genes *Mup7* and *Mup17* all showed a strong contribution from this mechanism, consistent with genes in multi-copy or paralogous genomic contexts where SNPs can reduce the alignment score gap between the correct locus and related sequences."),
("NEW-DEC/PHASE-K1","The second class showed a different pattern. At these genes, bias remained even when multi-mapping reads were included, consistent with direct SNP misalignment. The relevant reads may fail to align, may be assigned to another locus, or may remain multi-mappers on both references, so that fractional counting shifts both arms alike; in none of these cases does fractional counting rescue the signal. *Rps2*, *Rpl29*, and *Klra1* were clear examples of this class. For these genes, multi-mapping-inclusive counting is insufficient, and a strain-matched reference is needed to recover the FVB signal. Even then, a SNP-only reference does not resolve every locus: the decomposition of the bias into FVB/NJ-read gain and C57BL/6J-read loss shows that the personalised reference demonstrably recovers FVB/NJ reads at «decomp_both|d» of the «catalog_n|d» catalog genes, and at *Rps2* in skin it recovers none under unique-only counting."),
("CORR","This distinction is useful in practice. If investigators cannot immediately implement personalised reference alignment, multi-mapping-inclusive counting may reduce bias for some genes, particularly those in the SNP-induced multi-mapping class. It is less useful for direct-misalignment genes. The personalised FVB reference is more general because it improves the match between FVB reads and the target locus before counting. We therefore regard personalised reference alignment as the preferred correction strategy when genes of interest appear in the catalog, while multi-mapping-inclusive counting can serve as a secondary sensitivity analysis. These results suggest a simple workflow for FVB RNA-seq studies. Investigators should first record the FVB substrain and the reference assembly used for alignment. They should then check whether primary genes of interest appear in the catalog (Supplementary Table S2) or have high FVB/NJ SNP density. If a vulnerable gene is central to the study conclusion, the analysis should be repeated with a strain-matched or personalised reference where possible, and the direction and magnitude of any reference-dependent change should be reported."),
("NEW-ASM/PHASE-K1", v2par("discussion.md","A natural question is why we quantified").replace("where the SNP-only reference recovers no FVB/NJ reads,","where the SNP-only reference recovers no FVB/NJ reads under unique-only counting,")),
("NEW-NC", v2par("discussion.md","The B6-congenic PyVT tumours also serve")),
("NEW-PD","A methodological lesson of this study concerns the public data themselves. We verified every sample against the GEO/SRA metadata and, independently, against the FVB-allele fraction in its aligned reads; the sequence-based check is cheap (minutes per sample on the naive alignment that the pipeline produces anyway) and it is the only step that tests the one assumption on which a strain comparison rests. In our hands it both confirmed the identity of all «qc_used_rows|d» animals finally used and rejected a candidate dataset whose four \"wild-type C57BL/6J\" animals carried fixed non-B6 haplotype blocks on several chromosomes, consistent with an incompletely backcrossed background (GSE55792; Supplementary Fig. S1); the same scan at full resolution also revealed a residual heterozygous donor-strain segment on chromosome 16 in every tumour of the B6-congenic PyVT line we used as a negative control. Neither observation is visible in the metadata, and neither is a criticism of the studies concerned, whose designs did not depend on the point; but for any analysis that depends on the strain of publicly deposited samples we recommend treating a sequence-based identity check as a mandatory quality-control step and reporting its results, as we do in Supplementary Table S1."),
("NEW-WS", v2par("discussion.md","Reference-genome choice could in principle also affect")),
("CORR/R1-M2","This study has several limitations. First, the FVB/NJ personalised reference used here included only SNP substitutions and did not incorporate indels or structural variants (SVs). This occurred because the MGP REL-2021 indel VCF used FILTER=\".\" rather than FILTER=\"PASS\", and all indels were therefore excluded by the --pass option during g2gtools VCI construction (Supplementary Note 1). The bias estimates reported here should therefore be interpreted as conservative estimates of SNP-mediated reference-genome mapping bias, not as estimates of additional bias caused by indels or SVs. The true underestimation of FVB expression may be larger, particularly at loci enriched for indels or SVs. Future studies using a fully personalised reference that incorporates FVB/NJ- or FVB/N-specific variants would allow a more complete correction. Second, the personalised reference was built from FVB/NJ variants; the pure-strain animals are all FVB/NJ, but the F1 animals of GSE200632 are FVB/N and the substrain of the GSE293440 cross is not recorded. Third, the systematic survey was based on public GEO metadata and therefore captures what was reported rather than every undocumented local analysis step. Fourth, the analysis used public RNA-seq datasets that differ in tissue, library type, sequencing design, and experimental context. This diversity is useful for testing reproducibility but limits control over technical variables. Fifth, we focused on gene-level counts. Transcript isoform estimates, allele-specific splicing, untranslated regions, and paralog-specific expression may have additional bias patterns that were not fully evaluated here. Sixth, the SNP-patched reference was not compared with the FVB_NJ_v3 genome assembly [CITE Wong 2012]; whether the catalog loci behave the same way against the full assembly remains to be tested."),
("CORR","Taken together, these results indicate that B6-reference alignment does not broadly distort FVB RNA-seq data. Most genes are essentially unaffected. A small group of SNP-dense loci, however, shows reproducible and sometimes large underestimation of FVB expression and should be interpreted carefully. For studies focused on catalog genes, we recommend FVB/NJ personalised reference alignment when appropriate, noting that at the most SNP-dense loci a SNP-only reference is not sufficient. At minimum, investigators should report the reference genome used, screen genes of interest against the catalog and SNP-density information, and perform a sensitivity analysis when a vulnerable locus is central to the biological conclusion."),
(KEEP,"The same framework can be applied to other mouse strains. FVB is not unique in this respect: any non-B6 strain may show reference-genome mapping bias when aligned to a B6-based assembly. The scale of the problem will depend on genetic distance from B6, SNP density at expressed loci, and the genomic structure of the genes under study. Wild-derived strains such as CAST/EiJ and PWK/PhJ are likely to show broader effects than FVB because they carry more extensive sequence divergence from the B6 reference [CITE Keane 2011, Lilue 2018]. Even among classical inbred strains, the same logic applies. A strain-aware pipeline and a reproducible bias catalog can help distinguish true biological expression differences from reference-dependent measurement artifacts."),
("H","## Conclusions"),
("R2-C2","This study defines the scale and genomic distribution of SNP-mediated reference-genome mapping bias in FVB RNA-seq. The bias is not genome-wide, but is concentrated, reproducible, and enriched at SNP-dense loci. The «catalog_n|d»-gene catalog and three-arm alignment framework provide a practical approach for designing, interpreting, and reanalysing FVB RNA-seq studies. The catalog and the bias estimates were derived with Jackson Laboratory FVB/NJ animals and the MGP FVB/NJ variant set, and FVB/N-derived substrains (FVB/NTac, FVB/NCrl, FVB/NJcl and others) and FVB-background transgenic lines carry additional private variation, so the catalog should be applied with the substrain in mind and, where possible, re-derived with a matched variant set. Because the whole analysis rests on the strain of each public sample being what its record says, we also recommend that a sequence-based strain-identity check be reported for every sample in studies of this kind. By improving the use of existing public datasets and helping investigators avoid unnecessary follow-up experiments driven by mapping artefacts, this framework also supports Reduction and analytical Refinement in animal research."),
]
section("discussion.md", disc)
