L=open(f"{D}/figure_legends.md", encoding="utf-8").read()
def v2legend(title_start):
    for x in re.split(r"\n\s*\n", L):
        if x.strip().startswith("**"+title_start): return x.strip()
    raise KeyError(title_start)
def v2legend_body(title_start):
    parts=[x.strip() for x in re.split(r"\n\s*\n", L)]
    for i,x in enumerate(parts):
        if x.startswith("**"+title_start): return parts[i+1]
    raise KeyError(title_start)
leg=[("H","# Figure Legends"),
("H","**Figure 1. Public FVB RNA-seq studies use B6 reference genomes without strain-aware correction.**"),
("CORR","**(A)** Reference genome usage among «survey_total|d» public GEO series reporting FVB RNA-seq data. Studies were classified as B6 reference stated, reference not reported, or strain-aware alignment used. B6 reference included GRCm38/mm10, GRCm39/mm39, or legacy mm9. Among the «survey_total|d» series, «survey_stated_B6_n|d» reported a B6 reference genome, «survey_not_reported_n|d» did not report a reference genome, and none reported a strain-aware alignment strategy. No study reported WASP, g2gtools, personalised genome construction, or N-masking. **(B)** Schematic of SNP-mediated reference-genome mapping bias. FVB reads that span strain-specific SNPs may align poorly, fail to align, or be assigned incorrectly when mapped to the B6 reference. This reduces the apparent FVB signal in the naive arm. Alignment to the personalised FVB reference improves recovery of these reads at the corresponding loci."),
("H", v2legend("Figure 2.")), ("NEW-F1/PHASE-K", v2legend_body("Figure 2.").replace("(heterozygous in every F1 animal)","(expected to be heterozygous in every F1 animal)")),
("H","**Figure 3. FVB/NJ-personalised reference alignment reduces locus-specific bias without shifting the genome-wide distribution.**"),
(KEEP,"In panel C, the displayed standard errors summarize variability across expressed genes within each dataset-level arm."),
("CORR","**(A)** Per-gene scatter plot of log₂FC (FVB/B6) under naive B6 alignment and FVB-reference alignment. GSE123894 liver is shown as a representative dataset. Each point represents one expressed gene. The dashed diagonal marks equality between the two arms. Points above the diagonal have positive bias, meaning that naive B6 alignment underestimates FVB expression. Catalog genes are coloured by the number of qualifying non-tumour tissues. **(B)** Violin plots of per-gene bias across the four tissues. Bias was defined as log₂FC_FVB ref − log₂FC_naive. Circles mark the median; triangles mark the 95th percentile. The genome-wide median was within «gw_median_bias_absmax|.3f» log₂ units of zero in every tissue. **(C)** Dataset-level mean log₂FC (FVB/B6) ± standard error for the three alignment arms. WASP filtering removed «wasp_removed_min_pct|.2f»–«wasp_removed_max_pct|.2f»% of assigned reads per group. Reads with no vW tag and reads with vW=1 were retained, whereas reads with a vW tag other than 1 were removed. Genome-wide mean log₂FC under WASP differed from naive alignment by at most «gw_wasp_minus_naive_absmax|.3f» log₂ units. C57BL/6J animals lost more reads than FVB/NJ animals in every tissue (Supplementary Table S4). FVB-reference alignment also remained close to the naive arm at the genome-wide level."),
("H","**Figure 4. SNP-dense loci are enriched among genes vulnerable to SNP-mediated reference bias.**"),
("CORR","**(A)** Per-gene reference-mapping bias plotted against FVB/NJ SNP density. SNP density was calculated as SNPs per kilobase of gene body. Grey points indicate expressed genes. The «catalog_n|d» catalog genes are highlighted as green stars. *Ndufs5* and *Rps2* are labelled. The inset shows that «pred_catalog_in_top_quartile|d» of «catalog_n|d» catalog genes fall in the top quartile of genome-wide SNP density. **(B)** Per-gene bias plotted against gene length. Gene length is shown on a log₁₀ scale. Spearman correlation and p-value are shown in the panel. **(C)** Per-gene bias plotted against mean expression level. Mean expression was calculated as log₂ mean CPM in the naive arm. The red line shows the binned mean ± standard error across 20 equal-percentile bins. Bias was not associated with transcript abundance beyond a weak negative correlation. **(D)** Distribution of per-gene bias by gene biotype. Violin plots show protein-coding genes, lncRNAs, pseudogenes, miRNAs, and other genes. Median bias was within ±«pred_biotype_median_absmax|.3f» of zero in all biotype categories; the Kruskal–Wallis p-value is shown inset."),
("H","**Figure 5. A catalog of «catalog_n|d» genes consistently biased across non-tumour tissue datasets.**"),
("CORR","Panel A shows the top 30 genes for display; the complete «catalog_n|d»-gene catalog is provided in Supplementary Table S2. Because mean bias was calculated across expressed non-tumour tissues, a catalog gene can have a mean bias below 0.5 even if it met the criterion of bias ≥ 0.5 in at least two tissues."),
("CORR","**(A)** Heatmap of per-tissue bias values for the top 30 of «catalog_n|d» catalog genes. Genes are sorted by the number of qualifying non-tumour tissues and then by mean bias. Columns show adipose tissue, hypothalamus, liver, and skin. Bias was defined as log₂FC_FVB ref − log₂FC_naive. Grey cells indicate genes not expressed above the quantification threshold. **(B)** Dot plot of all «catalog_n|d» catalog genes. The x-axis shows the number of non-tumour tissues with bias ≥ 0.5 log₂ units. The y-axis shows mean bias across the tissues in which the gene was tested. Point colour indicates gene biotype. Point size is proportional to mean bias. The eight four-tissue genes with the highest mean bias and all genes with mean bias ≥ 3 are labelled."),
("H", v2legend("Figure 6.").replace("not recovered by the SNP-only personalised reference)","not recovered by the SNP-only personalised reference under unique-only counting)")), ("NEW-WE/PHASE-K1", v2legend_body("Figure 6.").replace("not because FVB/NJ reads are recovered, so","not because FVB/NJ reads are recovered under unique-only counting, so")),
("H", v2legend("Supplementary Figure S1.")), ("NEW-ID", v2legend_body("Supplementary Figure S1.")),
("H","**Supplementary Figure S2. Multi-mapping sensitivity analysis reveals two mechanisms of SNP-mediated reference-genome mapping bias.**"),
("CORR","**(A)** Genome-wide distribution of Δ across «mm_n_pairs|,d» expressed gene-tissue pairs from the four non-tumour tissues. Δ was defined as bias_multi − bias_unique. Bias_multi was calculated after including multi-mapping reads with fractional assignment. Bias_unique was calculated with the standard unique-only pipeline. The x-axis is truncated at ±1 log₂ unit for display. The dashed line marks Δ = 0. The distribution is centred near zero, indicating that multi-mapper exclusion did not create a global bias. Catalog gene-tissue pairs are shown as coloured points above the histogram. **(B)** Comparison of unique-only bias and multi-mapping-inclusive bias for the «catalog_n|d» catalog genes. Each point represents one catalog gene using the mean over tested tissues. The dashed diagonal marks equality. Points below the diagonal have lower bias after multi-mapping reads are included. Genes are coloured by mechanism class. **(C)** Residual fraction for each catalog gene. Residual fraction was defined as bias_multi / bias_unique. Lower values indicate that more of the unique-only bias was reduced by multi-mapping inclusion. Genes are sorted by residual fraction. **(D)** Per-tissue comparison of unique-only bias and multi-mapping-inclusive bias for four representative catalog genes chosen by rule. *Ndufs5* and *Mup17* represent the SNP-induced multi-mapping class. *3000002C10Rik* and *Rps2* represent the direct SNP misalignment class. Tissues in which the gene was not expressed above the quantification threshold are omitted."),
("H", v2legend("Supplementary Figure S3.")), ("NEW-DEC", v2legend_body("Supplementary Figure S3.")),
]
