note1=[("H","# Supplementary Note 1. Failure of g2gtools BAM coordinate liftover and its resolution"),
("H","## Background"),
(KEEP,"After aligning FVB/NJ reads to the personalised FVB reference genome (Arm 3), the intended workflow was to use `g2gtools convert -f bam --reverse` to project the resulting BAM alignments back into B6 (GRCm39) coordinates, after which the B6 Ensembl 110 GTF could be applied uniformly for featureCounts quantification. This note documents why that liftover step produced empty output BAMs for every dataset, explains the underlying cause, and describes the validated alternative strategy used throughout the analysis."),
("H","## Observed failure"),
("CORR","`g2gtools convert -f bam --reverse` applied to FVB-aligned BAMs reports every alignment as \"Cannot Map\", for paired-end and single-end libraries alike, and the output BAMs contain zero alignments by `samtools flagstat`. The failure is universal and is not a limitation specific to any dataset or library type."),
("H","## Cause: SNP-only VCI has no coordinate offsets"),
(KEEP,"The VCI (variant chain index) constructed by `g2gtools vcf2vci` for this study encodes only SNP substitutions. Because the MGP REL-2021 indel VCF carries FILTER=\".\" rather than FILTER=\"PASS\" for all records, the `--pass` flag caused all indels to be silently excluded (see *Methods: FVB/NJ personalised reference genome construction*)."),
(KEEP,"`g2gtools convert -f bam --reverse` translates each read's alignment position by applying the positional offsets stored in the VCI. These offsets are generated only by insertions and deletions: an insertion in the FVB genome shifts all downstream coordinates forward relative to B6; a deletion shifts them backward. With a SNP-only VCI, there are no positional offsets at any locus. Every read's FVB alignment position is therefore numerically identical to its B6 position. The converter correctly reports \"Cannot Map\" for every read because no applicable coordinate transformation exists in the VCI for any position in the genome."),
("H","## Resolution"),
("CORR","Because FVB and B6 coordinates are identical under the SNP-only VCI, no liftover step is required. FVB-aligned BAMs can be quantified directly using the B6 GTF. In this analysis the Ensembl 110 B6 GTF is used for the FVB-reference arm at both stages: it is supplied to STAR at mapping time (`--sjdbGTFfile`) exactly as in the naive arm, and it is the annotation given to featureCounts. The naive and FVB arms of every animal therefore differ only in the genome sequence (B6 versus SNP-patched FVB/NJ) and in the WASP options of the naive arm; the annotation, the counting mode and all other parameters are identical. The g2gtools-converted FVB/NJ GTF is used for one purpose only: the catalog filter that requires a gene to have a well-defined FVB/NJ locus (present in the converted GTF). Of the «gtf_genes_total|,d» genes of Ensembl 110, «gtf_genes_not_in_fvb|,d» («gtf_genes_not_in_fvb_pct|.1f»%) are absent from the converted GTF; all of them have zero FVB/NJ SNPs in the gene body, confirming that these are annotation-boundary rather than divergence-driven gaps."),
(KEEP,"The resulting count matrix is directly comparable with the naive and WASP arm count matrices without any coordinate adjustment."),
("H","## The effective splice-junction set of the FVB-reference arm"),
("CORR", v2sec("supplementary_note1.md","The effective splice-junction set of the FVB-reference arm")[0]),
("H","## Summary"),
("CORR","The `g2gtools convert -f bam --reverse` liftover is non-functional when the VCI contains only SNPs, because SNPs produce no coordinate offsets. The failure is universal across both paired-end and single-end datasets and is a consequence of the indel exclusion described in the Methods, not a limitation specific to paired-end alignment. The correct approach, used for every animal and arm in this study, is to quantify FVB-aligned BAMs directly with the B6 GTF, which is permissible because FVB and B6 coordinate systems are numerically identical under a SNP-only VCI; the same B6 GTF defines the splice junctions of both arms at mapping time."),
]
section("supplementary_note1.md", note1)
note2=[("H","# Supplementary Note 2. Multi-mapping sensitivity analysis: mechanistic decomposition of SNP-mediated reference-genome mapping bias"),
("H","## Purpose"),
(KEEP,"Standard RNA-seq quantification pipelines — including the featureCounts runs used throughout this study — exclude reads that map to two or more loci with equal alignment score (multi-mapping reads). SNP-mediated reference-genome mapping bias can arise through two distinct mechanisms, and the distinction is informative about the locus-level biology:"),
("CORR/PHASE-K1","**Mechanism 1. Direct SNP misalignment.** FVB reads spanning variant positions incur mismatch penalties in the B6 reference alignment that are severe enough to prevent alignment entirely, or that cause the read to be assigned to another locus. These reads are absent from the alignment, assigned to a non-cognate locus, or multi-mappers on both references, so that fractional counting shifts the strain ratio on both references alike; in none of these cases does fractional counting change the bias, which is therefore unaffected by whether multi-mapping reads are included or excluded from quantification. The two classes are defined operationally by |Δ| (Methods); the names describe the usual mechanism, not every member."),
("CORR","**Mechanism 2. SNP-induced multi-mapping.** FVB reads spanning variant positions incur SNP mismatch penalties that narrow the alignment score gap between the true locus and one or more paralogous loci. When the scores become equal, STAR classifies the read as a multi-mapper (MAPQ < 255) and featureCounts places it in the Unassigned_MultiMapping category. The same reads map uniquely and with full score to the FVB-reference arm, where no mismatch penalty applies. When multi-mapping reads are included with fractional assignment (−M −−fraction), they are partially returned to the naive arm counts, reducing the apparent bias; the FVB arm is largely unaffected because FVB reads already map uniquely there. Negative Δ (bias_multi − bias_unique) is the signature of this mechanism."),
("H","## Methods"),
("CORR","For each tissue, separate multi-mapping-inclusive featureCounts count matrices were generated using `featureCounts -M --fraction -s 0` (plus `-p` for the paired-end datasets GSE123875, GSE123893, GSE123894) applied to:"),
("CORR","- **Naive multi arm**: B6-reference-aligned BAMs of all animals (same BAMs as the primary naive arm).\n- **FVB multi arm**: FVB-reference-aligned BAMs of all animals (same BAMs as the primary FVB arm)."),
(KEEP,"Bias under multi-mapping inclusion was computed as:"),
(KEEP,"$$\\text{bias}_\\text{multi} = \\log_2\\text{FC}_{\\text{FVB multi}} - \\log_2\\text{FC}_{\\text{naive multi}}$$"),
("CORR","using the same edgeR models as the primary analysis (`~ diet + strain`, or `~ treatment + sex + strain` for skin). The per-gene quantity:"),
(KEEP,"$$\\Delta = \\text{bias}_\\text{multi} - \\text{bias}_\\text{unique}$$"),
(KEEP,"measures how much bias changes when multi-mapping reads are included. The residual fraction is defined as:"),
(KEEP,"$$\\text{residual\\_fraction} = \\text{bias}_\\text{multi} / \\text{bias}_\\text{unique}$$"),
("CORR","A value near 1 indicates bias that persists under multi-mapping inclusion (consistent with direct misalignment); a value near 0 indicates bias that is largely resolved by multi-mapper inclusion (consistent with SNP-induced multi-mapping). Negative values arise when multi-mapping reads restore so much FVB signal in the naive arm that the apparent bias reverses sign (consistent with strong multi-mapping from related loci). Catalog genes were classified on the mean over tested tissues."),
("H","## Genome-wide results"),
("CORR","Across «mm_n_pairs|,d» tested gene × tissue pairs from the four tissues, multi-mapping inclusion changed the median bias by only «mm_median_delta|+.3f» log₂ units (mean Δ = «mm_mean_delta|+.3f»). Only «mm_pct_abs_gt_0.5|.1f»% of gene × tissue pairs showed |Δ| > 0.5 log₂ and «mm_pct_abs_gt_1|.1f»% |Δ| > 1. This confirms that multi-mapper exclusion does not introduce meaningful bias at the genome-wide level, and that the primary bias estimates and the null distribution (genome-wide median near 0) are not artefacts of the unique-only counting strategy."),
("H","## Catalog gene results"),
("CORR","Among the «catalog_n|d» catalog genes:"),
("CORR/PHASE-K1","- **«mech_multi_n|d» genes showed |Δ| > 0.3 log₂** (SNP-induced multi-mapping component, mean |Δ| = «mech_multi_mean_absdelta|.2f» log₂; residual fractions ranged from «mech_multi_residual_min|.2f» to «mech_multi_residual_max|.2f», the extremes being genes whose multi-mapping-inclusive bias reverses sign or exceeds the unique-only bias). In this group, «mech_multi_residual_le_0.30|d» genes had residual fractions at or below 0.30, meaning more than 70% of their unique-only bias is attributable to multi-mapping reads excluded from the standard pipeline. This class contains the most consistently biased catalog gene, *Ndufs5* (mean residual fraction «ex_robust_residual|.2f»), several ribosomal protein genes with processed pseudogene copies (*Rps18*, *Rpl34*, *Rpl3*, *Rpl35a*, *Rps28*), the major urinary protein genes *Mup7* and *Mup17* (a multi-copy family), and *Gm25939* (snRNA).\n- **«mech_direct_n|d» genes showed |Δ| ≤ 0.3 log₂** (direct SNP misalignment dominant, mean |Δ| = «mech_direct_mean_absdelta|.2f» log₂; residual fractions «mech_direct_residual_min|.2f»–«mech_direct_residual_max|.2f»). «mech_direct_residual_ge_0.80|w» of these had residual fractions ≥ 0.80, indicating that ≥ 80% of their unique-only bias persists even when multi-mapping reads are included. The clearest example is *Rps2* («ex_extreme_snp_density|.1f» SNPs/kb; residual fraction «ex_extreme_residual|.2f»), whose FVB/NJ reads remain multi-mappers among *Rps2* and its processed pseudogenes on both references, so that fractional counting shifts the strain ratio on both references alike (see The Rps2 skin case below); the MHC class I gene *H2-Q5*, by contrast, falls in the multi-mapping class, consistent with its many paralogues."),
("CORR","These classifications are reported in Supplementary Table S2 (columns `mean_bias_multi`, `delta_multi_minus_unique`, `residual_fraction`, `mechanism_class`) and shown in Supplementary Fig. S2."),
("H","## The Rps2 skin case"),
("NEW-WE/PHASE-K1","The skin *Rps2* bias («ex_extreme_skin_bias|+.2f» log₂ unique-only) is the largest single-tissue bias in the direct-misalignment class and is essentially unchanged by multi-mapping inclusion (bias_multi = «ex_extreme_skin_bias_multi|+.2f» log₂; residual fraction «ex_extreme_skin_residual|.2f»). The skin libraries are single-end 52-bp reads; at a locus with «ex_extreme_n_snps|d» FVB/NJ SNPs and «rps2_indels_gene_body|d» homozygous indels in «ex_extreme_length_kb|.1f» kb («rps2_snps_exonic|d» SNPs and «rps2_indels_exonic|d» indels within exons) a 52-bp read frequently carries two or more differences from the B6 sequence. The FVB/NJ reads are not absent: they are multi-mappers on both references. On the B6 reference they are assigned predominantly to two processed pseudogenes of *Rps2*, *Rps2-ps10* and *Rps2-ps13*; on the SNP-patched FVB/NJ reference they are divided almost equally among *Rps2* and the same two pseudogenes, so the patched *Rps2* matches them as well as the pseudogenes do, but not better, and they remain excluded from unique-only counts (table below; repository files `rps2_indels_pseudogenes.md` and `rps2_multimap_cpm.tsv`). Because fractional counting raises the FVB/NJ-to-C57BL/6J ratio of *Rps2* by a similar amount on both references (by «rps2_skin_naive_log2ratio_shift|.2f» and «rps2_skin_fvb_log2ratio_shift|.2f» log₂ units), the bias is unchanged when multi-mapping reads are included."),
("NEW-WE/PHASE-K1","Counts per million of *Rps2* and of the three processed pseudogenes that carry its multi-mapping reads in skin. Every value in this note and in the main text is the mean over the eight FVB/NJ or eight C57BL/6J animals, with every arm and counting mode divided by the naive-arm unique-only library size of the same animal (the normalisation of Fig. 6B); unique, unique-only counting; fractional, −M −−fraction."),
("NEW-WE/PHASE-K1","| Gene | B6 ref., FVB/NJ unique | B6 ref., FVB/NJ fractional | B6 ref., C57BL/6J unique | B6 ref., C57BL/6J fractional | FVB/NJ ref., FVB/NJ unique | FVB/NJ ref., FVB/NJ fractional | FVB/NJ ref., C57BL/6J unique | FVB/NJ ref., C57BL/6J fractional |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n"+"\n".join(f"| *{g.replace('_','-')}* | «rps2mm_{g}_naive_unique_FVB_cpm|.2f» | «rps2mm_{g}_naive_multi_FVB_cpm|.2f» | «rps2mm_{g}_naive_unique_B6_cpm|.2f» | «rps2mm_{g}_naive_multi_B6_cpm|.2f» | «rps2mm_{g}_fvb_unique_FVB_cpm|.2f» | «rps2mm_{g}_fvb_multi_FVB_cpm|.2f» | «rps2mm_{g}_fvb_unique_B6_cpm|.2f» | «rps2mm_{g}_fvb_multi_B6_cpm|.2f» |" for g in ("Rps2","Rps2_ps10","Rps2_ps13","Rps2_ps6"))),
("NEW-WE/PHASE-K1","Under unique-only counting the SNP-only personalised reference does not recover these reads either: the FVB/NJ counts per million are essentially unchanged across the three arms (Fig. 6B), the matched-reference bias in skin is «decomp_Rps2_skin_bias_matched|+.2f» log₂, and the «ex_extreme_skin_bias|+.2f» log₂ bias is entirely loss of the C57BL/6J reads, which carry the B6 alleles, on the FVB/NJ sequence (b6_loss «decomp_Rps2_skin_b6_loss|+.2f»; the same reads are removed by WASP in the WASP arm). Because the reads remain multi-mappers among *Rps2* and its pseudogenes on both references, and the indels of the FVB/NJ allele are not in the reference, the true FVB/NJ *Rps2* expression in skin is undetermined here. The practical consequence under a standard unique-only pipeline is a «ex_extreme_naive_fold|.0f»-fold apparent underexpression of a housekeeping gene in FVB/NJ skin that has no demonstrated biological basis; the lesson for correction is that a SNP-only personalised reference is not sufficient at this locus."),
("H","## Interpretation"),
("CORR/PHASE-K1","The multi-mapping sensitivity analysis reveals that the catalog gene list spans two mechanistic classes. Genes in the SNP-induced multi-mapping class («mech_multi_n|d»/«catalog_n|d») are typically members of multi-copy gene families or have closely related paralogous sequences in the genome; their bias is inflated in unique-only counts because SNP penalties reduce alignment specificity at the variant-overlapping read positions. Genes in the direct misalignment class («mech_direct_n|d»/«catalog_n|d») tend to carry very high SNP densities, such that reads with multiple SNP mismatches fail to align, are assigned to another locus, or remain multi-mappers on both references; short single-end reads exacerbate this class."),
("NEW-DEC/PHASE-K1", v2sec("supplementary_note2.md","Interpretation")[1].replace("the SNP-only reference recovers no FVB/NJ reads at all.","the SNP-only reference recovers no FVB/NJ reads at all under unique-only counting.")),
]
section("supplementary_note2.md", note2)
